@model ProductModel

@*
    asp-validation-summary="ModelOnly" shows errors that belong to the WHOLE form, not one field
    (added in code with ModelState.AddModelError("", "...")). Other options:
      "All"  = list every error here too,   "None" = show nothing.
*@
<div asp-validation-summary="ModelOnly"></div>

@*
    asp-for="Name" connects the tag to the ProductModel.Name property. From that ONE attribute it writes:
      <label for="Name">Name</label>                       (text = property name, or [Display(Name=...)])
      <input type="text" id="Name" name="Name" value="..." maxlength="50" data-val-...="..." />
        - name="Name"  -> on POST, model binding puts the value back into ProductModel.Name
        - value="..."  -> the current value, so the form is pre-filled on Edit and after an error
        - type         -> picked from the C# type (decimal -> text, bool -> checkbox, DateOnly -> date)
        - maxlength / data-val-*  -> copied from [StringLength], [Required], [Range] on the model
          (data-val-* is for client-side validation; this demo doesn't load that script, so checks run on the server)
        - class="input-validation-error" -> added when this field has an error (CSS can turn it red)
    Why not write name="Name" by hand? A typo (name="Nmae") compiles fine and silently loses the value.
    asp-for="Nmae" is a COMPILE error, because it's checked against the Product class.
*@
<label asp-for="Name"></label>
<input asp-for="Name" />
@*
    asp-validation-for="Name" shows the error message for that one property, e.g. "Name is required.".
    Empty when there's no error. The message text comes from the attribute on the model.
*@
<span asp-validation-for="Name" class="field-error"></span>

<label asp-for="Category"></label>
@*
    On a <select>: asp-for sets name/id and marks the matching option as selected.
    asp-items gives the list of <option>s. SelectList turns our string[] into options:
        <option>Computers</option> <option>Furniture</option> <option>Lighting</option>
    The hand-written "-- choose --" option stays first (value="" -> [Required] fails if picked).
*@
<select asp-for="Category" asp-items="@(new SelectList((string[])ViewBag.Categories))">
    <option value="">-- choose --</option>
</select>
<span asp-validation-for="Category" class="field-error"></span>

<label asp-for="Price"></label>
<input asp-for="Price" />
<span asp-validation-for="Price" class="field-error"></span>

<label asp-for="Description"></label>
@* On a <textarea>, asp-for does the same job: name, id, current text, maxlength. *@
<textarea asp-for="Description"></textarea>
